WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
INTRO:
For decades, Intel was the king of semiconductors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.120
Their performance was unmatched and consumers&nbsp;
were proud to have an Intel powered machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.640
Even 5 years ago, Intel maintained much&nbsp;
of this same dominance as they simply had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.400
no competition. But with time, it seems like&nbsp;
Intel’s brand power and market share are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.920
being eroded away year after year. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
they’re competitors are doing better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.600
ever before. AMD has been consistently posting&nbsp;
all time high market share and Nvidia nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.080
reached a trillion dollar valuation. Not to&nbsp;
mention, we’ve been experiencing the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:42.240
chip shortage of all time over the past 2 years.&nbsp;
So, if Intel was ever going to get back on track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.200
it was during the past 2 years. But, Intel&nbsp;
has been struggling to even fully recover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:55.040
In fact, Intel is still down 37% from their dotcom&nbsp;
high which was set 22 years ago. In that same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:01.680
period, AMD has grown 3X and Nvidia has grown&nbsp;
70X. And if you needed any more confirmation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.960
that investors have no faith in Intel, you just&nbsp;
have to take one look at their PE ratio which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:14.560
currently sits at 7.35. For perspective, even&nbsp;
Toyota has a PE ratio above 10. And Toyota is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:25.840
the slow moving automotive industry, not the semi&nbsp;
conductor industry. So, what happened to Intel?

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.560
MISSING OUT ON MOBILE:
One of Intels’ first missteps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.000
was missing out on the mobile market. In the&nbsp;
late 2000s and early 2010s, smartphones were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.600
just starting to gain popularity. And contrary to&nbsp;
popular to belief, Intel was actually on top of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:45.200
this. In fact, they started development of a chip&nbsp;
called the Atom in 2004 which was specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.240
designed for small internet-connected devices. And&nbsp;
the first Atom chip called the Silverthrone came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.240
out soon after the first iPhone in 2008. But while&nbsp;
Intel did realize the potential of small devices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.360
they vastly underestimated the potential of&nbsp;
smartphones. You see, when Apple was developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.000
the iPhone, they actually approached Intel and&nbsp;
asked them to be their chip provider. And Intel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.880
would spend some time doing volume, cost,&nbsp;
and revenue projections. But, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.560
Intel would turn down Apple thinking that&nbsp;
producing chips for the iPhone wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.000
be worth the effort. Clearly, Intel vastly&nbsp;
underestimated the popularity of the iPhone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.600
and Intel’s former CEO, Paul Otellini was actually&nbsp;
super upfront about this shortcoming. He stated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.360
quote “in hindsight, the forecasted cost was wrong&nbsp;
and the volume was 100x what anyone thought.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.080
While it’s great that Paul was able to acknowledge&nbsp;
their shortcoming, this doesn’t change the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.320
that Intel not only missed out on Apple, but&nbsp;
all of the major smartphone manufacturers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.160
Samsung and Xiaomi to Oppo and Huawei. This gave&nbsp;
Qualcomm the perfect opportunity to step in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.960
dominate the sector. Intel would eventually come&nbsp;
around to this market and they would get the atom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.480
chip into smartphones even from Samsung and LG.&nbsp;
But, Intel never became the primary chip provider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.960
for any of these companies not just because&nbsp;
they were late but because they didn’t produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.680
ARM architecture chips. If you’re not familiar&nbsp;
with ARM architecture chips, these are basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.400
the standard within the mobile industry. The key&nbsp;
difference between ARM chips and Intel chips is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.680
their power draw. ARM chips are specifically&nbsp;
designed to be as power efficient as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.600
while Intel chips are generally optimized&nbsp;
to offer as much performance as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.160
Intel took a gamble with performance over&nbsp;
efficiency, and this was definitely the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.600
move given that 95% of smartphones are powered&nbsp;
with ARM based chips today. Intel tried to hang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.960
on as long as possible, but they would eventually&nbsp;
give up in 2016 after losing a major contract.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.240
While Intel had decisively lost out on the&nbsp;
smartphone chip market, they did spot one more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.760
opportunity within the mobile market and that was&nbsp;
5g modems. Intel was actually rather early to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.720
market and they were even in talks with Apple&nbsp;
to become their primary 5g modem producer. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.520
the problem was that Apple wasn’t entertaining&nbsp;
Intel’s offer because Intel was their top choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.640
but because they had issues with Qualcomm. And&nbsp;
unfortuantely for Intel, Apple and Qualcomm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.120
settled their differences right before Intel’s&nbsp;
partnership was about to begin. Intel could’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.760
tried to compete against Qualcomm, but they&nbsp;
didn’t really want to do this, so they would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.440
up selling their 5G modem business to Apple for $1&nbsp;
billion. And before you knew it, Intel had missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.920
out on the first wave of smartphones, the second&nbsp;
wave of smartphones, and the race towards 5G.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:24.480
AMD:&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.280
Aside from missing out big on the smartphone&nbsp;
market, Intel would also face serious competition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.200
within their core market from none other than&nbsp;
AMD. This was definitely something that Intel did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.880
not see coming. From their perspective, they had&nbsp;
won the desktop market. They had loads of deals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.160
all over the place even including Apple. But,&nbsp;
this would all change when a woman named Lisa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:52.000
Su joined AMD in 2012. Lisa knew that Intel&nbsp;
had a massive lead in terms of technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:58.640
not to mention, AMD was nearly bankrupt. So, the&nbsp;
only way AMD could catch up was by risking it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:04.080
and that’s exactly what she did. One of the core&nbsp;
characteristics of all semiconductor companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.800
is the Tick Tock model. This is when companies&nbsp;
switch between improving the chip itself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.440
improving the manufacturing process. Improvements&nbsp;
to both of these are crucial in producing more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.800
powerful chips, but companies will never attempt&nbsp;
to do both of them at the same time. Doing both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.880
at the same time makes troubleshooting errors&nbsp;
extremely difficult because the error could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.080
due to the new manufacturing process or due to the&nbsp;
new chip. Despite this, Lisa chose to ditch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.760
Tick Tock model and just try to improve both sides&nbsp;
at the same time. For years, we had little idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:39.520
as to how well this process was going, but when&nbsp;
Ryzen and Epyc were launched in 2017, it was clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:45.280
that AMD had hit it out of the park. AMD had not&nbsp;
only caught up to Intel in terms of technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.640
but they had actually outdone them especially&nbsp;
in terms of multi tasking. For the same price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.840
Ryzen offered significantly more cores and threads&nbsp;
than Intel. Now of course, each of these cores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.480
were less powerful than Intels, but for many&nbsp;
tasks such as streaming, having more cores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.560
was preferable to having more powerful cores.&nbsp;
But, AMD was just getting started. Lisa’s goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.920
wasn’t just to beat Intel but leave them in the&nbsp;
dust, and that’s exactly what they’ve done. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.320
of the clearest signs of this was how quickly AMD&nbsp;
was able to transition to the next architecture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.080
If you’re not familiar with chips, they’re&nbsp;
usually classified by a size such as 7 nm or 10 nm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.280
which is indicative of their fabrication process.&nbsp;
The long term goal is to make these sizes as small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:34.080
as possible and let’s just say AMD smoked Intel to&nbsp;
10 nm chips. AMD launched their 7 nm architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.680
3000 series chips in mid 2019. Meanwhile, Intel&nbsp;
didn’t complete their transition to 10 nm till&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:45.520
early 2021. Now, it should be noted that AMD’s 7&nbsp;
nm architecture is roughly equivalent to Intel’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:51.520
10 nm architecture, but this still means that&nbsp;
AMD beat Intel to market be roughly 18 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.840
All of this has made AMD the top choice for&nbsp;
desktop users. In fact, AMD overtook Intel in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:04.240
terms of desktop CPU marketshare in early 2021. At&nbsp;
this point, the only reason Intel is still hanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.440
on is because they have so many deals with laptop&nbsp;
and PC makers, and gamers still often prefer Intel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:14.640
due to superior single core performance. But, if&nbsp;
Intel doesn’t make moves against AMD right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:17.440
it’s likely that this market&nbsp;
will slowly dissipate as well.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:21.760
STAGNATION:&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.160
The rise of the mobile market and stiff&nbsp;
competition from AMD were no doubt major external&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.200
threats to Intel’s business. But, honestly, AMD&nbsp;
likely wouldn’t have been able to catch up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.280
even humiliate Intel if Intel was on top of their&nbsp;
game, and truth is, Intel simply wasn’t. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.400
if you want proof, just take a look at the GPU&nbsp;
market. Lisa has advanced AMD exponentially within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.640
the GPU space as well, but they still can’t quite&nbsp;
catch up to Nvidia. Now, don’t get me wrong here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.840
AMD GPUs have become a solid option especially&nbsp;
from a price to performance perspective. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.720
if we simply look at the most powerful GPUs on&nbsp;
the market, there’s no question that Nvidia is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.520
the clear winner. And the key reason for this&nbsp;
is that Nvidia and Jensen Huang were always on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:06.720
their A game. So, when AMD starting throwing&nbsp;
punches with their Radeon and Vega GPUs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.840
Nvidia was well prepared to fight back.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, Intel was simply caught off guard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:16.480
and one of the main reasons for this is Intel’s&nbsp;
leadership crisis. Intel’s leadership issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.760
can be traced back to 2005 when Paul Otellini&nbsp;
took over. Paul was actually the first CEO of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:27.440
Intel that was not an engineer. He had joined the&nbsp;
company’s sales and marketing division in 1974,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:32.000
and he slowly worked his way up from there. Paul’s&nbsp;
lacking technological background was actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.280
major concern when he took over. But he appeased&nbsp;
much of the skeptics with phenomenal revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.760
Paul was a master marketer and deal maker which&nbsp;
allowed Intel to become more dominant than ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.320
before within the desktop market. With that&nbsp;
being said though, it could be argued that Paul’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.560
lack of technological experience resulted in him&nbsp;
making poor fundamental decisions for the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.760
For example, he didn’t embrace ARM based chips. I&nbsp;
feel that someone with a technological background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:00.400
like Jensen Huang or Lisa Su would’ve made&nbsp;
this jump in a heartbeat. While Paul may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.280
have made some bad technological moves, at least&nbsp;
he blew up Intels’ profits and revenue. Intel’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.560
next CEO Brian Krzanich didn’t do this either.&nbsp;
Intel’s revenue basically just went sideways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:15.120
throughout all of his leadership. This makes sense&nbsp;
as most of Brian’s leadership was just filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.520
pulling out of the smartphone market. The worst&nbsp;
part, however, was the drama that Brian brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.560
with him. You see, in 2018, it would be revealed&nbsp;
that Brian was having an affair with an employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:30.240
and this would get him fired. Bob Swan would&nbsp;
replace him as CEO, and unfortunately for Bob,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.840
this was terrible timing. He inherited Intel right&nbsp;
as AMD was doubling down on Ryzen which meant that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:40.480
Bob had to take all the heat for falling behind.&nbsp;
And it was under Bob’s leadership that Intel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.280
struggled to transition to 10 nm chips. To make&nbsp;
things even worse, it was under his leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:51.040
that Apple ditched Intel. As you would guess, Bob&nbsp;
didn’t last very long and he was quickly replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:56.400
by Patrick Gelsinger in early 2021. Fortunately,&nbsp;
Patrick Gelsinger does have a technical background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:01.680
and he’s been with Intel for over 40 years. So,&nbsp;
hopefully, he’s the right person to turn Intel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.560
around. But, there’s no doubt that the last decade&nbsp;
of Intel’s leadership wasn’t the most productive.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.720
THE FATE OF INTEL:&nbsp;

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:16.080
Looking forward, Intel is no risk of going&nbsp;
bankrupt. They’re still pulling in 10s of billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:22.240
in profit every single year. But, with that being&nbsp;
said, Intel is not the best of positions either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:27.280
They’re not the dominant chip player that they&nbsp;
used to be. And looking forward, it seems more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:33.280
and more likely that Intel turns into something&nbsp;
like AT&amp;T, Cisco, or GE. While these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:38.480
are still massive, they’re not exactly exciting&nbsp;
or innovative. And in a fast moving industry such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:43.360
as semi conductors, it’s not clear how long Intel&nbsp;
can just remain dormant. The rest of the industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.280
has already eclipsed Intel several fold over the&nbsp;
past decade. Nvidia is currently worth 2.5 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.880
Intel, and that’s with Nvidia being down 50% from&nbsp;
their all time high. And earlier this year, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:01.200
AMD overtook Intel in terms of market cap which&nbsp;
was unthinkable just 5 years ago. Also, let’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:06.960
forget about the chip shortage. AMD’s profits grew&nbsp;
5X during the shortage, Qualcomm’s profits grew 4X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:12.560
during the shortage, and Nvidia’s profits grew 3X&nbsp;
during the shortage. Meanwhile, Intel’s net income&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:18.480
didn’t even move. And if Intel can’t catch up&nbsp;
during a chip shortage, it’s unclear if they ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.440
will. And, at this point, it seems like the best&nbsp;
case scenario for Intel is to just maintain their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:29.120
marketshare because the days Intel dominance are&nbsp;
long gone. Do you think Intel can recover? Comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:34.640
that down below. Also, drop a like if you’re glad&nbsp;
that AMD introduced competition into the market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

